This peer realizes the countable atomless Boolean algebra through binary-coordinate subsets of the natural numbers, relates its neighbourhood system to the universal domain, and connects Boolean entailment with inclusion of truth sets.

<!-- formalization -->

**Theorem (`exercise813a`).** The universal neighbourhood domain is domain-isomorphic to the domain built from finite binary level sets.

*Proof.* Use the established effective isomorphism between the two neighbourhood systems and forget the extra effectiveness data. ∎

**Theorem (`example_813_a`).** The universal neighbourhood domain and the binary level-set domain are domain-isomorphic.

*Proof.* This is the preceding isomorphism. ∎

**Theorem (`generator_nonempty`).** Every binary-coordinate generator is nonempty.

*Proof.* The number \(2^i\) has its \(i\)-th binary digit set. ∎

**Theorem (`generators_independent`).** For any finite set of coordinates and any prescribed Boolean values on them, the intersection choosing each generator or its complement accordingly is nonempty.

*Proof.* Form the bit mask of precisely those prescribed coordinates whose value is true. Its bits satisfy all the choices. ∎

**Theorem (`levelSet_compl`).** The complement of a binary level set of period \(2^k\) is the level set whose residue mask selects exactly the previously unselected residues.

*Proof.* A number lies in the complement exactly when the bit for its residue modulo \(2^k\) is absent from the original mask. ∎

**Theorem (`V.compl_mem`).** The complement of a neighbourhood in the binary level-set system is either empty or again a neighbourhood.

*Proof.* Rewrite the complement as a level set and distinguish whether that level set is empty. ∎

**Theorem (`testBit_mod_two_pow`).** The \(i\)-th bit of \(n\) modulo \(2^k\) equals the \(i\)-th bit of \(n\) when \(i<k\), and is false otherwise.

*Proof.* Division by \(2^k\) separates the low \(k\) binary digits from all higher digits. ∎

**Theorem (`affine_eq_biInter`).** For a residue \(\ell<2^k\), its congruence class modulo \(2^k\) is the intersection, over the first \(k\) coordinates, of the generator or its complement according to the corresponding bit of \(\ell\).

*Proof.* Two numbers below \(2^k\) are equal exactly when their first \(k\) bits agree, and reduction modulo \(2^k\) retains precisely those bits. ∎

**Theorem (`generatedBy_affine`).** Every congruence class modulo a power of two belongs to the Boolean algebra generated by the binary-coordinate generators.

*Proof.* Use its finite-intersection description and close the generated family under complements and intersections. ∎

**Theorem (`generatedBy_biUnion_affine`).** A finite union of congruence classes modulo \(2^k\), all with valid residues, belongs to the generated Boolean algebra.

*Proof.* Induct over the finite set of residues, using generation of each class and closure under union. ∎

**Theorem (`generatedBy_levelSet`).** Every binary level set belongs to the Boolean algebra generated by the coordinate generators.

*Proof.* A level set is the finite union of the congruence classes selected by its mask. ∎

**Theorem (`testBit_mod_two_pow_succ`).** Reducing a number modulo \(2^{i+1}\) preserves its \(i\)-th bit.

*Proof.* The coordinate \(i\) lies below \(i+1\), so the general reduction formula applies. ∎

**Theorem (`generator_eq_levelSet`).** Each coordinate generator is a level set of period \(2^{i+1}\), selecting precisely those residues whose \(i\)-th bit is one.

*Proof.* Reduction modulo \(2^{i+1}\) preserves the relevant bit. ∎

**Theorem (`V.mem_generator`).** Every coordinate generator is a neighbourhood of the binary level-set system.

*Proof.* Express it as a nonempty level set. ∎

**Theorem (`generatedBy_generator_imp`).** Every set in the Boolean algebra generated by the coordinate generators is either empty or a neighbourhood of the binary level-set system.

*Proof.* Induct through the generation rules. Generators and the whole space are neighbourhoods; finite intersections remain level sets, unions are closed, and complements are empty or neighbourhoods. ∎

**Theorem (`V.mem_iff_generatedBy`).** A set is a neighbourhood of the binary level-set system exactly when it is a nonempty member of the Boolean algebra generated by the coordinate generators.

*Proof.* Level sets are generated; conversely, the preceding induction says that a nonempty generated set must be a neighbourhood. ∎

**Definition (`Formula`).** A propositional formula is built from countably many variables, falsity, truth, negation, conjunction, and disjunction.

**Definition (`evalSet`).** The set interpretation sends a variable to its coordinate generator and interprets the Boolean connectives by the corresponding operations on subsets of the natural numbers.

**Definition (`evalV`).** The valuation interpretation assigns Boolean values to formulas by the usual truth tables.

**Definition (`SemanticEquiv`).** Two formulas are semantically equivalent when they have the same truth value under every Boolean valuation.

**Theorem (`exists_bitsOf_agree`).** Given finitely many variables and a Boolean valuation, there is a natural number whose binary digits agree with that valuation on all those variables.

*Proof.* Take the bit mask of the variables assigned true. ∎

**Definition (`formulaSetoid`).** Semantic equivalence is an equivalence relation on formulas.

**Definition (`Lindenbaum`).** The Lindenbaum algebra is the quotient of formulas by semantic equivalence.

**Theorem (`mem_evalSet_iff`).** A natural number belongs to the set interpretation of a formula exactly when the formula is true under the valuation given by that number's binary digits.

*Proof.* Induction on the formula follows the Boolean operations in the two interpretations. ∎

**Definition (`vars`).** The variables of a formula form the finite set obtained recursively from its variable occurrences.

**Theorem (`evalV_eq_of_agree`).** Two valuations that agree on every variable of a formula give that formula the same truth value.

*Proof.* Induction on the formula; compound formulas use agreement on the variables of each constituent. ∎

**Theorem (`semanticEquiv_iff_evalSet_eq`).** Two formulas are semantically equivalent exactly when their set interpretations are equal.

*Proof.* Semantic equivalence gives pointwise equality on binary valuations. Conversely, realize the finitely many variables of both formulas as bits of one natural number and use invariance under agreement on occurring variables. ∎

**Definition (`Lindenbaum.toSet`).** A semantic-equivalence class is sent to the common set interpretation of its representatives.

**Theorem (`Lindenbaum.toSet_mk`).** The set assigned to the class of a formula is its set interpretation.

*Proof.* This is the quotient construction applied to that representative. ∎

**Definition (`Entails`).** A formula entails another when every valuation satisfying the first also satisfies the second.

**Theorem (`Lindenbaum.toSet_injective`).** The set interpretation of the Lindenbaum algebra is injective.

*Proof.* Equal set interpretations imply semantic equivalence, so the representing formulas determine the same quotient class. ∎

**Theorem (`entails_iff_evalSet_subset`).** A formula entails another exactly when its set interpretation is contained in that of the other.

*Proof.* Inclusion immediately handles valuations arising from binary digits. For an arbitrary valuation, realize all variables occurring in either formula by one natural number and transfer both truth values along agreement. ∎

**Theorem (`example_813_b`).** Boolean entailment between formulas is exactly inclusion between their set interpretations.

*Proof.* This is the preceding entailment characterization. ∎
